            <ArticleTitle>Measuring noncalcified coronary atherosclerotic plaque using voxel analysis with MDCT angiography: phantom validation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>W242-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2214/AJR.07.2987</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This purpose of this study was to evaluate the accuracy and reproducibility of a voxel analysis technique for measuring noncalcified plaque in the coronary arteries.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIALS AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Polyethylene phantoms representing noncalcified plaque were scanned in both MDCT and micro-CT scanners and inter- and intrareader variability of volume calculation was performed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Volume measurements by both MDCT and micro-CT were comparable to the true volume as measured by micrometry (&lt; 3%, p = 0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There appears to be no significant difference (&lt; 3%) between MDCT and micro-CT measurements.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
